    <!-- TAB 4: SPEAK -->
    <div class="tab-content">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>Speak</h2>
            <p>This is the heart of the lesson — do all of it out loud. The timer is there to keep you going: fill the time with detail rather than escaping it.</p>

            <div class="note">
                <strong>Useful language:</strong> lean on today's passive — <em>"Frau Troffea was seized by an urge to dance"</em>, <em>"a stage was built"</em>, <em>"hundreds were affected"</em>, <em>"the cause has never been explained"</em>. For weighing theories, reach for: "One explanation is that…", "Critics argue that…", "What makes this so strange is…", "On balance, I'm inclined to think…".
            </div>

            <div class="timer">
                <span>Time remaining: <strong><span id="timer-display">4:00</span></strong></span>
                <div class="timer-controls">
                    <button class="duration-btn" onclick="setDuration(this, 3)">3 min</button>
                    <button class="duration-btn active" onclick="setDuration(this, 4)">4 min</button>
                    <button class="duration-btn" onclick="setDuration(this, 5)">5 min</button>
                    <button class="timer-btn" onclick="startTimer()">Start</button>
                    <button class="timer-btn secondary" onclick="resetTimer()">Reset</button>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task A · Tell the whole story (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>Tell the story of the Dancing Plague of 1518 in your own words, as if to someone who has never heard of it. Move through the arc: <em>a hungry, frightened Strasbourg → one woman dancing alone in the street → the outbreak spreading to hundreds → the doctors' "hot blood" cure and the disastrous stage → the shrine of Saint Vitus → the modern theories, and the mystery that remains.</em></p>
                <p>Use the <strong>passive voice</strong> at the key moments (<em>she was seized… a stage was built… hundreds were affected… the cause has never been explained</em>), and work in at least <strong>five key words</strong> (seize, compulsively, afflicted, hysteria, convulsions, purge, decree, toll, dread, haunt).</p>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task B · Which theory convinces you? (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>Weigh the two leading explanations — ergot poisoning versus mass psychogenic illness. Set out the evidence and the objections for each, then argue for the one you find more convincing. Is it more reassuring to believe a poison caused it, or more unsettling to accept that the mind alone could do this to a whole city?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="speak-prompt">
                <span class="speak-label">Task C · When crowds lose control (4 minutes)</span>
                <p>The Dancing Plague is often called an early case of mass hysteria. Talk about how emotions and behaviour spread through crowds. Use the passive to describe events whose cause is unclear.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:6px;"><strong>Stuck for an idea? Pick one of these and run with it:</strong></p>
                <ul style="margin:6px 0 0 20px; color:#3a2a5a;">
                    <li>A panic, craze or rumour that swept through a group of people you know of — how did it start and spread?</li>
                    <li>A moment at a concert, a match or a protest where a whole crowd seemed to act as one.</li>
                    <li>A "cure" or piece of expert advice that later turned out to be completely wrong.</li>
                    <li>A modern belief or online trend that spread far faster than the facts behind it.</li>
                    <li>A historical event that is well recorded yet still not fully explained — and why the mystery appeals to us.</li>
                    <li>A time fear or dread changed how a group of people behaved.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="final-discussion">
                <h3>To finish — the bigger questions</h3>
                <ol>
                    <li>The authorities acted on the best medical knowledge available and made things far worse. When is it more dangerous to act than to do nothing?</li>
                    <li>Mass psychogenic illness suggests the mind can produce real, physical symptoms in a crowd. How much does that idea change the way you think about "real" versus "imagined" illness?</li>
                    <li>Why do you think a strange, unexplained event from five hundred years ago still grips us more than many well-understood tragedies?</li>
                    <li><strong>Before you go — from memory:</strong> give three sentences about the Dancing Plague using the passive voice (one past simple, one present perfect, one with a modal like <em>can</em>), and explain why the passive fits each one.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

